[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507444 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d935e86d-2878-5713-88af-732c2cd4dd43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d935e86d-2878-5713-88af-732c2cd4dd43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507145} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb3c213e-a427-5f30-8b6a-3f5985900ded" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "62a29958-3d43-5575-a73a-6517a1734f8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-324461" :question/problem "The price of a deli sandwich is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ dollars and a standard deviation of $$1.10.$ In addition, in $90%$ of cases the price is between $$0.80\\mu $ and $$1.20\\mu .$ Find the average price, **rounding your answer to two decimal places**.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$\\mu ≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$dollars$" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent a sandwich price. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{1.10}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.80\\mu <X<1.20\\mu )=0.90.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.80\\mu <X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.90 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.90 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.45 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.5+0.45 \\\\ P(X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.95.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.95 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.20\\mu }{1.10}) & =0.95 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.20\\mu }{1.10}) & =0.95 \\\\ \\frac{0.20\\mu }{1.10} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.95).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.95)=1.6449.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.20\\mu }{1.10} & =1.6449 \\\\ 0.20\\mu  & =1.809\\,39 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{1.809\\,39}{0.20} \\\\  & ≈9.05\\,\\text{dollars}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507452 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33710c61-22d6-514d-a654-305c39257be0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33710c61-22d6-514d-a654-305c39257be0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506801} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dd58159-33f5-5db7-909d-d7a72f3ebd4b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7fe715dd-222c-5303-8e5d-661af680f9ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-314062" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we let\n\n$$\nL={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the formula for the instantaneous rate of change\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a},\n$$\n\nwe see that if we let $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ and $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{f(x)-f\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}}=L.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis confirms that $L={f}^{′}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$\n\nComputing the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\,{f}^{′}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\n$$\n\nand therefore, we conclude that $L=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507456 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "686fb959-8e23-5381-8bd9-2f970d41778e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "686fb959-8e23-5381-8bd9-2f970d41778e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506922} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fbfa6a5-6a10-5832-a94e-61877fd05794" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507328} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f5f8125-cec1-5372-9fa6-f054c0ec30d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f5f8125-cec1-5372-9fa6-f054c0ec30d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507020} :answer-field/id #uuid "b06e3cb5-1f20-5499-8cd9-ff51fcfab7bc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "88277403-5532-598b-ac5e-1cdba1716886" :question/math-academy-id "q-181353" :question/problem "Find the solution to the inequality $\\frac{5t-6}{2}\\le 1.$\n\nThe solution is $t$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5t-6}{2}\\le 1 \\\\ 2\\cdot (\\frac{5t-6}{2}) & \\le 2\\cdot 1 \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot (\\frac{5t-6}{\\cancel{2}}) & \\le 2 \\\\ 5t-6 & \\le 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5t-6 & \\le 2 \\\\ 5t-6+6 & \\le 2+6 \\\\ 5t & \\le 8 \\\\ \\frac{5t}{5} & \\le \\frac{8}{5} \\\\ t & \\le \\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $t\\le \\frac{8}{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507457 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46bfa4c3-af30-5756-82bb-059f1b23c4a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506843} {:answer/id #uuid "4850c323-0008-5586-a92c-51d27d9d80e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{25}{6})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506847} {:answer/id #uuid "9af9ba7a-b83f-5100-8ba8-0c8c301f0339" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507013} {:answer/id #uuid "aa4ea64c-142a-5719-b240-6ec5e8ce7a0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{5}{3})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507054} {:answer/id #uuid "b6500cbd-5e5a-59f1-8c25-13b96afe7d98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{5}{3})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46bfa4c3-af30-5756-82bb-059f1b23c4a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506843} :answer-field/id #uuid "f20bb066-c188-50d4-908b-5327e0942d6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8c418bae-22aa-5bc3-86d4-82df570c3162" :question/math-academy-id "q-78927" :question/problem "Factor ${x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{3}x+\\frac{25}{36}.$" :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{3}x+\\frac{25}{36}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{3}x+{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{6}x+{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=\\frac{5}{6}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31a95984-f3ff-5d40-aa45-92f765a76484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3s-1}{(s+1)(s-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31a95984-f3ff-5d40-aa45-92f765a76484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3s-1}{(s+1)(s-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506796} :answer-field/id #uuid "35230cec-1558-5848-ab5a-d11ccbfeb44d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507269} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e846976b-6bbf-5002-a11d-4c4ba08add2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{s+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e846976b-6bbf-5002-a11d-4c4ba08add2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{s+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507190} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6f7f1d9-3783-567e-8823-55b10da69ad9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9a8a2b45-1f2b-5de6-b95b-a5731dcdb607" :question/math-academy-id "q-331914" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=4{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,{X}_{2}(s)=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\} &=L\\{-{x}_{1}(t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\} &=L\\{4{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0) &=-L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0) &=4L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+2L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)+1 &=-{X}_{1}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-3 &=4{X}_{1}(s)+2{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$\n\nNow, solving the first equation for ${X}_{1}(s),$ we get\n\n$$\ns{X}_{1}(s)+1=-{X}_{1}(s)\\,⟹\\,{X}_{1}(s)=-\\frac{1}{s+1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the second equation and solving for ${X}_{2}(s),$ we have\n\n$$\ns{X}_{2}(s)-3=4(-\\frac{1}{s+1})+2{X}_{2}(s)\\,⟹\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{3s-1}{(s+1)(s-2)}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11c2fead-d108-57e7-8db0-525fce50e750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y>-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506713} {:answer/id #uuid "2a9dca56-0354-57b4-a7e8-d9a0ef2a39c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y<-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506778} {:answer/id #uuid "696f454f-a239-53c4-b608-6d6ad371cc2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y<9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506925} {:answer/id #uuid "bd5e7ebb-9abc-5d94-9362-95adeacf2f8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y>-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507095} {:answer/id #uuid "e177af7e-0bf6-5205-9657-9a0ed5250651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y>3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11c2fead-d108-57e7-8db0-525fce50e750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y>-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506713} :answer-field/id #uuid "78e5e08f-692a-52d5-9828-56a741cd6532" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9fd93fd8-c1db-5a78-b0d6-62c128a57e89" :question/math-academy-id "q-156057" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{4y-9}{15}<\\frac{y}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expressions by multiplying both sides of the inequality by the lowest common positive multiple of the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4y-9}{15} & <\\frac{y}{3} \\\\ 15\\cdot (\\frac{4y-9}{15}) & <15\\cdot (\\frac{y}{3}) \\\\ 4y-9 & <5y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-9 & <5y \\\\ 4y-9-5y & <5y-5y \\\\ -9-y & <0 \\\\ -9-y+9 & <0+9 \\\\ -y & <9 \\\\ \\frac{-y}{-1} & \\,\\,>\\,\\,\\frac{9}{-1} \\\\ y & >-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y>-9.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507471 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c822341-4e56-50fd-ba09-fc792dcce56b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=\\frac{1}{2},\\,b=\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506699} {:answer/id #uuid "240cb937-fa6f-5fbd-a8f5-c57634feafc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-\\frac{2}{3},\\,b=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506754} {:answer/id #uuid "2b854fe0-ef1d-5321-90f8-585a9d8b0017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-\\frac{2}{3},\\,b=-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506780} {:answer/id #uuid "6055db78-569e-51e4-9439-86946d33caf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=\\frac{3}{2},\\,b=-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506909} {:answer/id #uuid "82cdf392-a488-5d1c-b7fc-d8ed86588e7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=\\frac{3}{2},\\,b=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6055db78-569e-51e4-9439-86946d33caf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=\\frac{3}{2},\\,b=-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506909} :answer-field/id #uuid "521ab5d3-f6d1-53a5-a68c-844d600c98bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a26cf28c-df8d-52da-88d5-cd7c098392bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-4594" :question/problem "Find the values of the constants $a$ and $b$ that make the function $f(x)$ below continuous at $x=-1$ and $x=2.$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-5, & x\\le -1 \\\\ 2ax+3b, & -1<x\\le 2 \\\\ 4, & x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "At $x=-1,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x) & =f(-1) \\\\  & =-5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}(2ax+3b) \\\\  & =2a(-1)+3b \\\\  & =-2a+3b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) \\\\ -5 & =-2a+3b \\\\ 2a-3b & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, at $x=2,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(2ax+3b) \\\\  & =2a(2)+3b \\\\  & =4a+3b,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}4=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) \\\\ 4a+3b & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's solve the following system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2a-3b &=5 \\\\ 4a+3b &=4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding both equations we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6a & =9 \\\\ a & =\\frac{9}{6} \\\\ a & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can replace $a$ by $\\frac{3}{2}$ into the first equation. Therefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(\\frac{3}{2})-3b & =5 \\\\ 3-3b & =5 \\\\ -3b & =2 \\\\ b & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$ and $x=2$ for $a=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $b=-\\frac{2}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507475 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b4e37a9-9371-541d-b046-02330a84b683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b4e37a9-9371-541d-b046-02330a84b683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507015} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6100537-9c91-5945-8d50-4bf692d695a1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507346} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "656c88fd-0784-5c15-95e4-e68ff8d0009a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "656c88fd-0784-5c15-95e4-e68ff8d0009a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506918} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae484b73-c158-5737-8b58-c8813ea0f8cf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a763aa0a-1786-546d-877a-3f8cb8ed771a" :question/math-academy-id "q-181373" :question/problem "Find the solution to the inequality $\\frac{2-3p}{4}<2p+1.$\n\nThe solution is $p$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-3p}{4} & <2p+1 \\\\ 4\\cdot (\\frac{2-3p}{4}) & <4\\cdot (2p+1) \\\\ \\cancel{4}\\cdot (\\frac{2-3p}{\\cancel{4}}) & <4\\cdot (2p+1) \\\\ 2-3p & <8p+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-3p & <8p+4 \\\\ 2-3p-8p & <8p+4-8p \\\\ 2-11p & <4 \\\\ 2-11p-2 & <4-2 \\\\ -11p & <2 \\\\ \\frac{-11p}{-11} & \\,\\,>\\,\\,\\frac{2}{-11} \\\\ p & >-\\frac{2}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $p>-\\frac{2}{11}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507477 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1af436a9-9425-59bc-9879-b5a2d4148878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a\\le 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506726} {:answer/id #uuid "32818b35-b7cb-56f3-b1f3-4d1137800999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a\\le -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506799} {:answer/id #uuid "63abf159-1bb5-5a42-9daa-463b46621411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a\\ge 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506915} {:answer/id #uuid "a20e1ecc-b32b-5179-94f5-f6fcfeabd193" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a\\ge -6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507028} {:answer/id #uuid "fda207e8-8fff-5625-a4ee-cd07bda49aab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a\\ge 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a20e1ecc-b32b-5179-94f5-f6fcfeabd193" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a\\ge -6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507028} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ad7f7db-4030-5bba-a9d1-a46ba85090e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8e1188e-48d5-534b-bcfd-6a2d0af01921" :question/math-academy-id "q-156062" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{2a-4}{8}\\le \\frac{2a}{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expressions by multiplying both sides of the inequality by the lowest common positive multiple of the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2a-4}{8} & \\le \\frac{2a}{6} \\\\ 24\\cdot (\\frac{2a-4}{8}) & \\le 24\\cdot (\\frac{2a}{6}) \\\\ 3\\cdot (2a-4) & \\le 4\\cdot 2a \\\\ 6a-12 & \\le 8a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6a-12 & \\le 8a \\\\ 6a-12-8a & \\le 8a-8a \\\\ -12-2a & \\le 0 \\\\ -12-2a+12 & \\le 0+12 \\\\ -2a & \\le 12 \\\\ \\frac{-2a}{-2} & \\,\\,\\ge \\,\\,\\frac{12}{-2} \\\\ a & \\ge -6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a\\ge -6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507489 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea413de4-f41d-5bd6-8d46-90d7116bd177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "51.99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea413de4-f41d-5bd6-8d46-90d7116bd177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "51.99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507195} :answer-field/id #uuid "abe2d838-2efe-5d60-8dd7-942ce7ac6cc4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd63f548-8bbe-5de7-9356-fc14587f7fab" :question/math-academy-id "q-325117" :question/problem "The heights of oak trees in a park are normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ feet and a standard deviation of $8$ feet. Additionally, $25%$ of the trees are shorter than $45$ feet. Find the percentage of trees taller than $50$ feet. **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z).$*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.4090\\,$ | $\\,0.4052\\,$ | $\\,0.4013\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.4483\\,$ | $\\,0.4443\\,$ | $\\,0.4404\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.0\\,$ | $\\,0.4880\\,$ | $\\,0.4840\\,$ | $\\,0.4801\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of the trees are taller than $50$ feet." :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the height of a randomly selected tree. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{8}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>50).$  \n\nWe know that $P(X<45)=0.25.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<45) & =0.25 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{45-\\mu }{8}) & =0.25 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{45-\\mu }{8}) & =0.25 \\\\ \\frac{45-\\mu }{8} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.25)=-0.6745.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{45-\\mu }{8} & =-0.6745 \\\\ 45-\\mu  & =-5.396 \\\\ \\mu  & =50.396.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(50<X)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>50) & =P(Z>\\frac{50-50.396}{8}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-0.05) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.05) \\\\  & =1-0.4801 \\\\  & ≈51.99%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $51.99%$ of the trees are taller than $50$ feet."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507491 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be4173d4-2fab-56de-9d24-84bcde3b6c23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be4173d4-2fab-56de-9d24-84bcde3b6c23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507098} :answer-field/id #uuid "25247a53-b3d9-5d93-a5e2-eb10bd145f04" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507262} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04d3bc34-7b3f-57d7-be3b-ca7e45a1f25b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04d3bc34-7b3f-57d7-be3b-ca7e45a1f25b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506685} :answer-field/id #uuid "754b3a47-c518-5c04-b26c-a2ac39a75809" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "beefe539-fabd-5c70-98d6-47fa9012204c" :question/math-academy-id "q-181384" :question/problem "Find the solution to the inequality $\\frac{2z+9}{3}\\ge -z-1.$\n\nThe solution is $z$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $3:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2z+9}{3} & \\ge -z-1 \\\\ 3\\cdot (\\frac{2z+9}{3}) & \\ge 3\\cdot (-z-1) \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot (\\frac{2z+9}{\\cancel{3}}) & \\ge 3\\cdot (-z-1) \\\\ 2z+9 & \\ge -3z-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z+9 & \\ge -3z-3 \\\\ 2z+9+3z & \\ge -3z-3+3z \\\\ 5z+9 & \\ge -3 \\\\ 5z+9-9 & \\ge -3-9 \\\\ 5z & \\ge -12 \\\\ \\frac{5z}{5} & \\ge \\frac{-12}{5} \\\\ z & \\ge -\\frac{12}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z\\ge -\\frac{12}{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507499 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "825a3f94-5d5f-59b8-9d4e-7d0f168885a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z>-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506965} {:answer/id #uuid "9bfb137d-cdc8-5e43-84c6-ea0025793bde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z<24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507016} {:answer/id #uuid "a1a10480-cbf4-58a2-9e6b-17815d58cadd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z<-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507026} {:answer/id #uuid "b207b853-0fed-5188-a900-3bb18b72d272" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z>\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507070} {:answer/id #uuid "ef835f55-6239-5e48-941a-ae73fc43925e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z<-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1a10480-cbf4-58a2-9e6b-17815d58cadd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z<-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507026} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4490670-0ae0-5d23-9e69-8c96fae8a13a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507356}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c8a935c4-ddf1-51df-a83c-2659ff68e2ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-154041" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $4<-\\frac{z}{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "The variable $z$ is being *divided* by $6.$ To isolate $z$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-6$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\,\\, & <\\,\\,-\\frac{z}{6} \\\\ (-6)\\cdot 4\\,\\, & >\\,\\,(-6)\\cdot (-\\frac{z}{6}) \\\\ -24\\,\\, & >\\,\\,(-6)\\cdot (-\\frac{z}{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-24\\,\\, & >(-6)\\cdot (-\\frac{z}{6}) \\\\ -24 & >(\\cancel{-}6)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}\\frac{z}{6}) \\\\ -24 & >6\\cdot \\frac{z}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $6$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-24 & >6\\cdot \\frac{z}{6} \\\\ -24 & >\\frac{6z}{6} \\\\ -24 & >\\frac{\\cancel{6}z}{\\cancel{6}} \\\\ -24 & >z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the inequality with the variable on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nz<-24\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z<-24.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507503 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e90f66c1-50aa-5eec-9fca-bddb4bba7214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e90f66c1-50aa-5eec-9fca-bddb4bba7214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507194} :answer-field/id #uuid "41f396f4-a600-561c-a811-891b140e8ab6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507274} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5197d664-2d80-5cab-8f1a-7a61713e0053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5197d664-2d80-5cab-8f1a-7a61713e0053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506864} :answer-field/id #uuid "835c17f7-36ef-548e-8b46-b6d83fc2e63a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf4150db-c6d9-5a42-a567-768a11a0a924" :question/math-academy-id "q-171616" :question/problem "Find the solution to the inequality $\\frac{5z+2}{3}\\ge 4.$\n\n$z$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5z+2}{3} & \\ge 4 \\\\ 3\\cdot (\\frac{5z+2}{3}) & \\ge 3\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot (\\frac{5z+2}{\\cancel{3}}) & \\ge 12 \\\\ 5z+2 & \\ge 12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5z+2 & \\ge 12 \\\\ 5z+2-2 & \\ge 12-2 \\\\ 5z & \\ge 10 \\\\ \\frac{5z}{5} & \\ge \\frac{10}{5} \\\\ z & \\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z\\ge 2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507506 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adfdc82e-f387-5cca-a236-db6c3b237ae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "adfdc82e-f387-5cca-a236-db6c3b237ae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507062} :answer-field/id #uuid "909bfc96-ecbc-5d98-91c1-caba1a5f0edc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507330} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ced4f854-67c0-55a7-8eb2-2342cefa6036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ced4f854-67c0-55a7-8eb2-2342cefa6036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507128} :answer-field/id #uuid "e46faa19-8528-52c0-9d68-d774c89993c7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d5194de3-9348-5e70-86f0-2dc5eae75238" :question/math-academy-id "q-181399" :question/problem "Find the solution to the inequality $\\frac{y+2}{3}<\\frac{y+3}{2}.$\n\nThe solution is $y$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expressions by multiplying both sides of the inequality by the lowest common positive multiple of the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+2}{3} & <\\frac{y+3}{2} \\\\ 6\\cdot (\\frac{y+2}{3}) & <6\\cdot (\\frac{y+3}{2}) \\\\ 2\\cdot (y+2) & <3\\cdot (y+3) \\\\ 2y+4 & <3y+9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+4 & <3y+9 \\\\ 2y+4-3y & <3y+9-3y \\\\ 4-y & <9 \\\\ 4-y-4 & <9-4 \\\\ -y & <5 \\\\ \\frac{-y}{-1} & \\,\\,>\\,\\,\\frac{5}{-1} \\\\ y & >-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y>-5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507508 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4163fb4e-1673-56b1-9e81-b3f9b76bdcdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506831} {:answer/id #uuid "b513ed2d-2ca6-556b-9cdc-932a781ed446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507077} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc323db-b9be-5ba3-89bf-74f4864af6f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507097} {:answer/id #uuid "ef817cb8-b7c8-5338-9ccb-b25eb7ba3fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b513ed2d-2ca6-556b-9cdc-932a781ed446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507077} :answer-field/id #uuid "955e89a0-7f01-5af1-93d8-b8760f8a0f25" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507334} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29bab42e-19fd-5999-bca5-a7f709334255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506773} {:answer/id #uuid "7380ffe2-b480-549e-b9ff-55f23f096330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9x}{8\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506943} {:answer/id #uuid "c1abe79a-867b-5b61-b180-db62e3c89468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9x}{32\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507105} {:answer/id #uuid "db644471-744d-5974-86ca-8c2d68c380d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29bab42e-19fd-5999-bca5-a7f709334255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506773} :answer-field/id #uuid "e28d2851-cb92-5497-aeb3-e6113495aa58" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d683633f-d60f-57e1-b38c-1931a877a7fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-343914" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x),$ where the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $6$-periodic function $(L=3)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2$, we have ${\\omega }_{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{2}=\\omega ,$ the $n=2$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=2$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x)=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}))+x(-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″} & =-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{2}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,[-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{2}}]+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{2}}=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A &=-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{4\\pi }{3}B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=\\frac{9}{16\\pi } \\\\ B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=\\frac{9x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507512 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec708962-ad6c-5d78-85a9-f0cb925fa107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\ln\\left(\\frac{9}{7}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec708962-ad6c-5d78-85a9-f0cb925fa107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\ln\\left(\\frac{9}{7}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507201} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b282f9b-5bb1-5f62-87e7-540c34e64d8c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9591085-98d5-5c4b-b445-1e12f9553b30" :question/math-academy-id "q-342996" :question/problem "Find the real solution to the equation $8\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x=1.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x=\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above definition into the equation $8\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8(\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}) & =1 \\\\ 8({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) & ={e}^{x}+{e}^{-x} \\\\ 8{e}^{x}-8{e}^{-x} & ={e}^{x}+{e}^{-x} \\\\ 7{e}^{x} & =9{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply both sides of the equation by ${e}^{x}$ and solve the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\cdot 7{e}^{x} & ={e}^{x}\\cdot 9{e}^{-x} \\\\ {e}^{2x} & =\\frac{9}{7} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{7}) \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{7}) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{7})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only real solution is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{7}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507514 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08959185-7ae8-545f-a27e-c6317b14d655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506692} {:answer/id #uuid "3dd24afb-2e8a-5315-bf56-83e7629e3b50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})$ and $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506822} {:answer/id #uuid "42bb5a15-e42c-5fde-b8fd-dab42d65178f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506834} {:answer/id #uuid "8306f30c-7d39-5a1a-8a33-fa06c26a37c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506969} {:answer/id #uuid "b943a61a-5fba-553b-ba34-318ed7a58bfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42bb5a15-e42c-5fde-b8fd-dab42d65178f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506834} :answer-field/id #uuid "93460c70-4a67-59ae-ac0f-2d86b0582d6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc0cc655-da41-5f5b-b054-719fe2ce042d" :question/math-academy-id "q-9675" :question/problem "Find the real solutions to the equation $5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=5.$" :question/worked-solution "The definitions of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x$ are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}),\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above definitions into the equation  $5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}))-\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) & =5 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{e}^{x}+\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-x}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x} & =5 \\\\ 2{e}^{x}+3{e}^{-x} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply both sides of the equation by ${e}^{x},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\cdot (2{e}^{x}+3{e}^{-x}) & ={e}^{x}\\cdot 5 \\\\ 2{e}^{2x}+3 & =5{e}^{x} \\\\ 2{e}^{2x}-5{e}^{x}+3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in ${e}^{x}$ that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{e}^{x}-3)({e}^{x}-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n2{e}^{x}-3=0,\\,{e}^{x}-1=0\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{x}-3 & =0 &  & \\, & {e}^{x}-1 & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =\\frac{3}{2} &  & \\, & {e}^{x} & =1 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}) &  & \\, & x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ and $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})$ are the real solutions to the equation."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507519 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96a96d60-5df7-525f-b9d5-460b27317666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{s-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96a96d60-5df7-525f-b9d5-460b27317666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{s-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507005} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ea34c0d-6f70-52cb-8ac2-9e4d837fa8f2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507313} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b891e4e8-a932-5e13-8640-98de8fa2c1b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-2s-2}{(s-3)(s-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b891e4e8-a932-5e13-8640-98de8fa2c1b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-2s-2}{(s-3)(s-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507085} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1cf17fd-da35-507f-b449-a0c201f4cbdd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1650579-5a44-5386-9786-86764c11f1fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-331915" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=3{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,{X}_{2}(s)=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\} &=L\\{3{x}_{1}(t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\} &=L\\{-2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0) &=3L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0) &=-2L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-4 &=3{X}_{1}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+2 &=-2{X}_{1}(s)+{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$\n\nNow, solving the first equation for ${X}_{1}(s),$ we get\n\n$$\ns{X}_{1}(s)-4=3{X}_{1}(s)\\,⟹\\,{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{4}{s-3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the second equation and solving for ${X}_{2}(s),$ we have\n\n$$\ns{X}_{2}(s)+2=-2(\\frac{4}{s-3})+{X}_{2}(s)\\,⟹\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{-2s-2}{(s-1)(s-3)}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508043 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13ed44a6-241d-5838-9fc9-af9b6f83e61d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507622} {:answer/id #uuid "1578c936-8ad1-508e-aa56-b4990c519451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507624} {:answer/id #uuid "67abf3a2-af43-5891-a4b8-887a1c46a81b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507751} {:answer/id #uuid "7e517eaa-0474-52ae-938e-84dbfaa53de5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507784} {:answer/id #uuid "8dda24db-7965-57a0-84c8-bf571ddd300c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1578c936-8ad1-508e-aa56-b4990c519451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507624} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5d3c470-c959-5590-9ddc-7ea57ee65776" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0cddfc6e-37ce-5b87-9aa0-8b3fda1d2938" :question/math-academy-id "q-90392" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}},\\,x>1\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{2},$ compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+2X-1].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\text{E}[aX+bY]=a\\text{E}[X]+b\\text{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+2X-1] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+2\\text{E}[X]-\\text{E}[1] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+2\\text{E}[X]-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{3}{x})|}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(3-\\frac{3}{a}) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=3$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+2X-1] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+2\\text{E}[X]-1 \\\\  & =3+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}-1 \\\\  & =3+3-1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508063 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d33b258-c127-5e48-89c1-172347927c06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1a/1abbfb9c3aeaaeed74af4948b0c645384d6f20c04c23d435224b855cf1192e6f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507627} {:answer/id #uuid "2e2779e9-a749-57a4-aa3d-9cd1888dce16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1e/1e5ea7ddfefb6f1ef81e75c123c528327618ca73bc01df764d2cf3e9564835e1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507657} {:answer/id #uuid "43aa1de5-a93e-57fb-ba78-684b4247686a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bb/bb5ac2100e6106e62b930a9a0cb94d8ce315dc41c4e15a40c5e02f5c5cacfa82.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507702} {:answer/id #uuid "e23c2923-cf2d-5e5e-9c53-dfb0399ef7c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e7/e7e697fe18c032e596590c68a04f0b01893b44ade18c6fcc732a79bf12ebcf4a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507902} {:answer/id #uuid "fe180236-0e5b-5dc5-967b-2a36380c113d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/54/5430d057eaa68b600ceeecca978212314972653382754767470cca5115c0dca5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43aa1de5-a93e-57fb-ba78-684b4247686a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bb/bb5ac2100e6106e62b930a9a0cb94d8ce315dc41c4e15a40c5e02f5c5cacfa82.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507702} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6d8c285-32aa-5c3d-ae1d-8f1ac9cad4c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e3ff34b-d5cf-5bf6-ba34-eb80d066dc38" :question/math-academy-id "q-27525" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{4}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/a5/a5b088c08da85c389cb96add374186a898793ec23cf0a3e39270c292fe3d606d.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph by a factor of $\\frac{1}{4}.$ The $y$-intercept becomes $(0,\\frac{1}{4}),$ and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/d5/d563be7169471498c6e0193e3be8493ae6ba119fc3fc7b74b397454b65e6cf57.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508072 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36f81b7b-95bc-5070-8586-1cb395b236dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/84/84edd950272f87c19041041fdb5175dde4bd04ed62b71be16669c76d00d93b38.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507678} {:answer/id #uuid "3f66385e-14cc-547e-ba13-182e0f71ee18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f3/f373cb57189451d27eaed320639d5fdcb8a1fcf6f4b1b2087acc94b6d719e09c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507696} {:answer/id #uuid "89b0b47c-97bc-5ce1-a901-60ba28570aea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/14/1448fe064fcd0ee80b02f6370fdc79be41d3ad655b06738876c5c8ff0c1363bf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507796} {:answer/id #uuid "91c70715-5f59-54ca-ab8d-95c8c925b87f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d6/d620960e531620c0f2fb0ca3d171a6c0d0a6c45348eca914345cd40fd4675245.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507804} {:answer/id #uuid "c3b6b136-4626-540b-bc21-4bbc2d52ae54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f6/f6f93982fecfb664cfcc41a849ce14e5da