{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509755} {:answer/id #uuid "2da9d709-0215-57a2-8672-e6206c6adafe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509784} {:answer/id #uuid "7f765ddc-fd83-5d86-95d4-4600a92c5521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3n\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509967} {:answer/id #uuid "be5da26b-6371-53a4-9ac5-17f64b40bfc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510090} {:answer/id #uuid "c7412e64-4513-5280-aa0d-2cc6735ca7f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3n\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510106} {:answer/id #uuid "d5c4c7a7-3555-51c9-990c-07a246dd3fc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be5da26b-6371-53a4-9ac5-17f64b40bfc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510090} :answer-field/id #uuid "d46cd0be-3da6-58f1-a00a-a7431aa0897f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510344} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2832c392-61c0-50a2-8169-320867050a00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509771} {:answer/id #uuid "2eb52a18-f4ee-5b25-978c-7f06c34831dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2832c392-61c0-50a2-8169-320867050a00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509771} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9a5ddff-f037-5246-a150-e944038eee10" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510348} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02d02289-48c0-58d1-be12-9e4164bd26d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{72(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509701} {:answer/id #uuid "9be14ef7-ca17-5724-aae1-6e23ad9abc2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510013} {:answer/id #uuid "ce7ebafd-9c9a-5118-9114-ac71ef6b796a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{72(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510123} {:answer/id #uuid "e647d9d2-d801-509a-ac89-f72e19a7ccba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e647d9d2-d801-509a-ac89-f72e19a7ccba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510176} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6e028ab-a36c-5469-ba63-ab22fef00ac7" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d451e41-c662-5263-b3f7-8483ecaa8976" :question/math-academy-id "q-342964" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=-2{x}^{2}+4$ defined on $[-3,3).$  Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension of $G(x)=-4x$ from the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n-2{x}^{2}+4∼-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{72(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}}\nand\n{{field-3}}\non $R,$ we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n-4x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-4}}\\cdot {{field-5}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and we are already given the coefficients ${a}_{0}$ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{72(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-3,3),$\n\n- continuous on $[-3,3),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-3)=F(3),$ if we extend the value at $x=3$ using the formula $F(x)=-2{x}^{2}+4.$\n\nTherefore, the $6$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{continuous}$ and $\\text{piecewise smooth}$ on $R,$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{x}^{2}+4) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{72(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ -4x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{72(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ -4x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-24(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\ -4x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{24(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510401 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09c1d491-402e-555e-a666-7f7c4e87d6ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4.225-0.35\\theta -2{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509710} {:answer/id #uuid "78a7b057-e13c-59a5-9c17-b9e7479f7400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.45+0.7\\theta +4{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509944} {:answer/id #uuid "811cdd6d-776a-59e7-8f58-71af09a1c53a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4.225-0.7\\theta -2{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509970} {:answer/id #uuid "8f42f131-75fe-5641-ab8b-d247827641d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.225+0.35\\theta +2{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509996} {:answer/id #uuid "ff1fb752-a169-5228-a97e-05992483d175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8.45-0.7\\theta -4{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "811cdd6d-776a-59e7-8f58-71af09a1c53a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4.225-0.7\\theta -2{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509970} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f8fd110-202a-5667-bf26-f7d318d06cc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "67f89f72-de60-5983-9ba7-f68316fa9c35" :question/math-academy-id "q-159972" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,1),$  where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean.  Given that the likelihood function of the sample $0.3,\\,1.2,\\,-2.6,\\,0.4$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=\\frac{1}{4{\\pi }^{2}}\\,{e}^{\\,g(\\theta )},\n$$\n\nwhat is the expression for $g(\\theta )?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{x-\\theta }{\\sigma })}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}.$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =0.3+1.2+(-2.6)+0.4 \\\\  & =-0.7 \\\\ C & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(0.3)^{2}+(1.2)^{2}+(-2.6)^{2}+(0.4)^{2} \\\\  & =8.45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=4.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-4/2}\\,{e}^{-(8.45-2(-0.7)\\theta +4{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{\\pi }^{2}}\\,{e}^{\\,-4.225\\,-\\,0.7\\theta \\,-\\,2{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(\\theta )=-4.225-0.7\\theta -2{\\theta }^{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510406 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c7dbb34-59dd-5e81-a863-a5c9d70d0ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510014} {:answer/id #uuid "a417d5b7-1f44-5776-946d-4b89b7864438" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510030} {:answer/id #uuid "a7c2e494-cb43-5ca0-8464-d42be445ccc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510042} {:answer/id #uuid "c5953b51-f0b1-5657-955a-863eedbda8b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510102} {:answer/id #uuid "c59ab5af-5a91-5ef6-bd69-64c38848927c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c59ab5af-5a91-5ef6-bd69-64c38848927c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510103} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9d445ab-f7a7-5bd0-ba53-a5e0545e4523" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f844afb-5020-5b9d-a059-ebc3dcd000de" :question/math-academy-id "q-117360" :question/problem "![](images/08/08f6351b8b95c87386d07466b6e876d72f33bc00034e83b360d03d23633d363d.png)\n\nWe need to create a cylindrical tin with a lid using $6\\pi \\,{ft}^{2}$ of sheet metal. What is the radius $r$ of the cylinder that has the largest volume?" :question/worked-solution "First, let $r$ be the radius and $h$ be the height of the cylinder.\n\nWe wish to maximize the volume\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nWe know that the surface area is fixed at $6\\pi \\,{cm}^{2},$ so we also have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n2\\pi rh+2\\pi {r}^{2}=6\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi rh & =6\\pi -2\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ h & =\\frac{6\\pi -2\\pi {r}^{2}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ h & =\\frac{3-{r}^{2}}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $V,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(r) & =\\pi {r}^{2}(\\frac{3-{r}^{2}}{r}) \\\\ V(r) & =(3r-{r}^{3})\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $V,$ so we need to differentiate $V$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(r)=(3-3{r}^{2})\\pi .\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ (3-3{r}^{2})\\pi  & =0 \\\\ 3 & =3{r}^{2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =1 \\\\ r & =1\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that, in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the radius $r$ must be positive.\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=1$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative ${V}^{″}(r)$ is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(r)=-6\\pi r,\n$$\n\nand we can see that ${V}^{″}(1)<0.$ So, the stationary point $r=1$ is indeed a maximum of $V(r).$\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the largest volume has a radius of $r=1\\,\\text{ft}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0abcfa87-c2d8-55b9-8b6c-f6e6dc2c2e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509713} {:answer/id #uuid "185a6029-68c4-5828-8b11-ace521807408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509734} {:answer/id #uuid "986be587-2ecf-570e-a688-8d2405f238d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510006} {:answer/id #uuid "dadc21a4-2efd-53e9-80c6-696e78b43e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510154} {:answer/id #uuid "fd82aff3-659c-5501-969a-3e3bd0a5021f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "986be587-2ecf-570e-a688-8d2405f238d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510006} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ef94707-58e0-523c-a81d-81716ae5304a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "904e690d-cf3b-5b36-80f3-f72dec8825a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-86220" :question/problem "A manufacturer wishes to build a cylindrical container with a base but no lid. The container must have an exact volume of $72\\pi \\,{ft}^{3}.$\nTo make the container, the manufacturer uses two materials:\n\n- a plastic sheet that costs $$1.5$ per ${ft}^{2}$ for the lateral surface,\n\n- a plastic sheet that costs $$0.5$ per ${ft}^{2}$ for the base.\n\nWhat is the radius of the container that gives the smallest manufacturing cost?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/95/959050ee306a96db3745f84bc7095d4e83a23ceadd819f0b749c04b20d5778d2.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the manufacturing cost. We can express the manufacturing cost $C$ in terms of $r$ and $h$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi rh\\cdot 1.5+\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =3\\pi rh+0.5\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $72\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n72\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constant equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{72}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(r) & =3\\pi r(\\frac{72}{{r}^{2}})+0.5\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{216\\pi }{r}+0.5\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $C,$ so we need to differentiate $C$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{216\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+\\pi r.\n$$\n\n \n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{216\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+\\pi r & =0 \\\\ \\pi r & =\\frac{216\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ \\pi {r}^{3} & =216\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =216 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{216} \\\\ r & =6\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<6$ then ${C}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>6$ then ${C}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=6$ is indeed a minimum of $C(r).$\n\nTherefore, the container with the smallest manufacturing cost has radius $r=6\\,\\text{ft}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510427 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b4a6c19-602c-54ee-8152-81351ed0e7e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509741} {:answer/id #uuid "c4704d4c-6962-5e51-9c39-966e80ebd0ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510100} {:answer/id #uuid "d1cdea9b-fce1-5271-9532-93edde53a638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510134} {:answer/id #uuid "d7cff4ee-3f14-55d3-aab1-becaa9d5c30e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510146} {:answer/id #uuid "f62f9599-77a5-5414-840d-7de4098038df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f62f9599-77a5-5414-840d-7de4098038df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510214} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7e8ebb0-e3d6-56f0-bb35-2dd8e698b8c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c0fb2ee-81ac-5d75-ab41-5ee64f815cef" :question/math-academy-id "q-117348" :question/problem "We wish to construct a cylindrical can with a base and a lid. Our cylinder must have an exact volume of $2\\,000\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ What is the radius of the cylinder that gives the smallest surface area?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/33/33c90b487d2b3036068acf4b771d2ca6ae95e8e8446b706b85fed058dc12a0bf.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi rh+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $2\\,000\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n2\\,000\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{2\\,000}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(r) & =2\\pi r(\\frac{2\\,000}{{r}^{2}})+2\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\,000\\pi }{r}+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $S,$ so we need to differentiate $S$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{4\\,000\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{4\\,000\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 4\\pi r & =\\frac{4\\,000\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{3} & =4\\,000\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =1\\,000 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{1\\,000} \\\\ r & =10\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<10$ then ${S}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>10$ then ${S}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=10$ is indeed a minimum of $S(r).$\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the smallest surface area has radius $r=10\\,\\text{cm}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510441 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4e759ae-9980-5d3f-9810-8593734c7be3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4e759ae-9980-5d3f-9810-8593734c7be3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510076} :answer-field/id #uuid "329ea85a-eb09-5964-bdf1-0a1e6d289646" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac9bf5e8-0b9b-5a4e-90d8-e2b158eca7f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-345153" :question/problem "We wish to construct a cylindrical can with a base but no lid. Our cylinder must have an exact volume of $8\\pi \\,{in}^{3}.$ What is the smallest possible surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ of the cylinder? Do not round the answer.\n\n${S}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{in}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/7f/7fe5dad64f7c065e693ed407e0f291ec995b4823a0356e11055d20a0ca89a53b.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi rh+\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $8\\pi \\,{in}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n8\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{8}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(r) & =2\\pi r(\\frac{8}{{r}^{2}})+\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{r}+\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $S,$ so we need to differentiate $S$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{16\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+2\\pi r.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{16\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+2\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 2\\pi r & =\\frac{16\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 2\\pi {r}^{3} & =16\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =8 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\ r & =2\\,\\text{in}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<2$ then ${S}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>2$ then ${S}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=2$ is indeed a minimum of $S(r).$\n\nFinally, to find the smallest surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}},$ we substitute $r=2\\,\\text{in}$ into the equation for the surface area $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\text{min}}=S(2) & =\\frac{16\\pi }{2}+\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\  & =8\\pi +4\\pi  \\\\  & =12\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510450 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e139e6d-04b2-50d6-80c2-c3eaf743883f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509851} {:answer/id #uuid "6fc53d03-6d9e-5293-ba20-93007fa4827a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509916} {:answer/id #uuid "735a2653-a0e7-558e-8ab6-95a5bb2ed37e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509929} {:answer/id #uuid "89e398d3-c766-5417-baa4-109f6c2e3d4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509987} {:answer/id #uuid "d6da261c-3a8c-54a5-8d8c-d5b40017cbd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510144}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89e398d3-c766-5417-baa4-109f6c2e3d4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509987} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f669200-1574-50fb-be9a-48d96ef7fd94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c69ae447-8c86-5d03-83d6-e7f16c525735" :question/math-academy-id "q-16231" :question/problem "![](images/86/8689a80bf3fddc055072d11837cd5bf2a6b4e966d935816fa1e8b68454774d24.png)\n\nWe need to create a cylindrical tin with no lid using $64\\pi \\,{cm}^{2}$ of a metal sheet. What is the radius $r$ of the cylinder that has the largest volume?" :question/worked-solution "First, let $r$ be the radius and $h$ be the height of the cylinder.\n\nWe wish to maximize the volume\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nWe know that the surface area is fixed at $64\\pi \\,{cm}^{2},$ so we also have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n2\\pi rh+\\pi {r}^{2}=64\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi rh & =64\\pi -\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ h & =\\frac{64\\pi -\\pi {r}^{2}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ h & =\\frac{64-{r}^{2}}{2r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $V,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(r) & =\\pi {r}^{2}\\times \\frac{64-{r}^{2}}{2r} \\\\ V(r) & =\\frac{(64r-{r}^{3})\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $V,$ so we need to differentiate $V$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(r)=\\frac{(64-3{r}^{2})\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{(64-3{r}^{2})\\pi }{2} & =0 \\\\ 64 & =3{r}^{2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{64}{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that, in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the radius $r$ must be positive.\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative ${V}^{″}(r)$ is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(r)=\\frac{-6\\pi r}{2}=-3\\pi r,\n$$\n\nand we can see that ${V}^{″}(\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3})<0.$ So, the stationary point $r=\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ is indeed a maximum of $V(r).$\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the largest volume has a radius of $r=\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,\\text{cm}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510451 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc4354de-3fdb-5904-a007-8872deb32b34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "302" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc4354de-3fdb-5904-a007-8872deb32b34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "302" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510118} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9f0d32e-11c5-5d16-b8fb-323736868b33" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7823091-db8e-5d0b-aebf-c2f82fe402a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-362573" :question/problem "We wish to construct a cylindrical can with a base and a lid. Our cylinder must have an exact volume of $128\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}.$ What is the smallest surface area we can get? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{ft}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/bb/bb9c356aadbb184aaa49c1fa1867da82d64ef40db9048c752c2a91d20704392a.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi rh+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $128\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n128\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{128}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(r) & =2\\pi r(\\frac{128}{{r}^{2}})+2\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{256\\pi }{r}+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $S,$ so we need to differentiate $S$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{256\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{256\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 4\\pi r & =\\frac{256\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{3} & =256\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =64 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{64} \\\\  & =4\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<4$ then ${S}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>4$ then ${S}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=4$ is indeed a minimum of $S(r).$\n\nFinally, to find the smallest surface area, we substitute $r=4\\,\\text{ft}$ into the equation for the surface area $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(4) & =\\frac{256\\pi }{4}+2\\pi (4)^{2} \\\\  & =301.592… \\\\  & ≈302\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible surface area of the cylinder is $S=302\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510452 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "072eda89-f2fd-5e6b-8865-e919b2deb069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509707} {:answer/id #uuid "6acfb0da-27b6-5749-b6e8-62595ebc8056" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509908} {:answer/id #uuid "7cce974d-ff76-5e44-b28b-aec2865278ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509957} {:answer/id #uuid "9557cf45-32ca-5990-b9e7-8944ea966b53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510002} {:answer/id #uuid "a3b55a48-54cb-5cad-b04e-d24581a88559" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9557cf45-32ca-5990-b9e7-8944ea966b53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510002} :answer-field/id #uuid "36df5534-c619-54f6-902b-ac3c15f3e762" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ca2de93c-f55b-512e-9e27-ab6ff5bd3cb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-96041" :question/problem "$$\n\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{A}=\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}^{Q}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}^{R}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{22}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{6}{12}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{A}=\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{Q}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{22}=(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a58aaf0f-44ae-5462-9b2b-e39f22326505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a58aaf0f-44ae-5462-9b2b-e39f22326505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510035} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fd7852a-49d4-5728-a9f3-6239cb8db233" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dcb7a44b-6317-5e52-8a59-4cac3f8ca2cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-345211" :question/problem "A manufacturer wishes to build a cylindrical container with a base but no lid. The container must have an exact volume of $\\frac{27}{4}\\pi \\,{in}^{3}.$\nTo make the container, the manufacturer uses two materials:\n\n- a plastic sheet that costs $$6$ per ${in}^{2}$ for the lateral surface,\n\n- a plastic sheet that costs $$1.50$ per ${in}^{2}$ for the base.\n\nWhat is the radius $r$ of the container that gives the smallest manufacturing cost? Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{in}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/f3/f32cf2ca384f0f1b676e77e74dd4f02484f2ba585710fee88df6951607081a42.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the manufacturing cost. We can express the manufacturing cost $C$ in terms of $r$ and $h$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi rh\\cdot 6+\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot 1.50 \\\\  & =12\\pi rh+1.5\\pi {r}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $\\frac{27}{4}\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n\\frac{27}{4}\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{27}{4{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(r) & =12\\pi r(\\frac{27}{4{r}^{2}})+1.5\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{81\\pi }{r}+1.5\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $C,$ so we need to differentiate $C$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{81\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+3\\pi r.\n$$\n\n \n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{81\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+3\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 3\\pi r & =\\frac{81\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 3\\pi {r}^{3} & =81\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =27 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<3$ then ${C}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>3$ then ${C}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=3$ is indeed a minimum of $C(r).$\n\nTherefore, the container with the smallest manufacturing cost has radius $r=3\\,\\text{in}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510468 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "217e50a3-d416-5fd8-8e38-c8d5e7424bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "217e50a3-d416-5fd8-8e38-c8d5e7424bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509757} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dc3b0ba-69af-5706-afb5-6c4ce41ddf74" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e97a779e-762a-58e2-bd98-1b5dcb7b5e80" :question/math-academy-id "q-345215" :question/problem "A manufacturer wishes to build a cylindrical container with a base but no lid. The container must have an exact volume of $\\frac{125}{3}\\pi \\,{yd}^{3}.$\nTo make the container, the manufacturer uses two materials:\n\n- a plastic sheet that costs $$7.50$ per ${yd}^{2}$ for the lateral surface,\n\n- a plastic sheet that costs $$2.50$ per ${yd}^{2}$ for the base.\n\nWhat is the radius $r$ of the container that gives the smallest manufacturing cost? Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{yd}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/55/55de2c06ea57724513e450cbf49a0477d63cd75bac601d5d95f0ba7e366852ff.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the manufacturing cost. We can express the manufacturing cost $C$ in terms of $r$ and $h$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi rh\\cdot 7.50+\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot 2.50 \\\\  & =15\\pi rh+2.5\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $\\frac{125}{3}\\pi \\,{\\text{yd}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n\\frac{125}{3}\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{125}{3{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(r) & =15\\pi r(\\frac{125}{3{r}^{2}})+2.5\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{625\\pi }{r}+2.5\\pi {r}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $C,$ so we need to differentiate $C$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{625\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+5\\pi r.\n$$\n\n \n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{625\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+5\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 5\\pi r & =\\frac{625\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 5\\pi {r}^{3} & =625\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =125 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{125} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<5$ then ${C}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>5$ then ${C}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=5$ is indeed a minimum of $C(r).$\n\nTherefore, the container with the smallest manufacturing cost has radius $r=5\\,\\text{yd}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510469 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c46be2e-d5f1-5ef8-a289-7d723f2d3602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt[3]{2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509744} {:answer/id #uuid "352d0b77-d507-5fa8-9984-728f0f7b7643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509801} {:answer/id #uuid "4b86d54b-8785-555f-8b97-1ffd9f11afa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{5}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509842} {:answer/id #uuid "5db31cea-417d-52b7-9fbf-ffb47cd2996b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509883} {:answer/id #uuid "89374edb-8117-5e96-8a69-ceaff369f502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5db31cea-417d-52b7-9fbf-ffb47cd2996b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509883} :answer-field/id #uuid "34501908-abbd-572a-a01d-25d855b9ad7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ebb08e7a-2131-5a36-9f64-7c4d80a5d168" :question/math-academy-id "q-85731" :question/problem "A manufacturer wishes to build a cylindrical tank with a base but no lid. The tank must have an exact volume of $100\\pi \\,{m}^{3}.$\nTo make the tank, the manufacturer uses two materials:\n\n- a plastic sheet that costs $$2.5$ per ${m}^{2}$ for the lateral surface,\n\n- a plastic sheet that costs $$2$ per ${m}^{2}$ for the base.\n\nWhat is the radius of the tank that gives the smallest manufacturing cost?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/67/67c54d0a678e9ea9d02919abacf9b048778de19e3d0367669455439583b7a767.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the manufacturing cost. We can express the manufacturing cost $C$ in terms of $r$ and $h$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi rh\\cdot 2.5+\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5\\pi rh+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $100\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n100\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constant equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{100}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(r) & =5\\pi r(\\frac{100}{{r}^{2}})+2\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{500\\pi }{r}+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $C,$ so we need to differentiate $C$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{500\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r.\n$$\n\n \n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{500\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 4\\pi r & =\\frac{500\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{3} & =500\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =125 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{125} \\\\ r & =5\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<5$ then ${C}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>5$ then ${C}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=5$ is indeed a minimum of $C(r).$\n\nTherefore, the container with the smallest manufacturing cost has radius $r=5\\,\\text{m}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510472 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bfc9292-582a-5130-b5d1-81e60b9f908c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "286\\pi \\,{mm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509778} {:answer/id #uuid "3aa62b7e-897f-5805-9918-dddd9fd86aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "294\\pi \\,{mm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509815} {:answer/id #uuid "b20f290b-3e64-5a30-80ba-4390b357a9ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "424\\pi \\,{mm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510073} {:answer/id #uuid "d9abe97b-7da5-5c8a-875a-77732d84b70b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "324\\pi \\,{mm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510150} {:answer/id #uuid "febe6f3b-813e-5306-859b-3fbeaedf67f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "306\\pi \\,{mm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3aa62b7e-897f-5805-9918-dddd9fd86aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "294\\pi \\,{mm}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509815} :answer-field/id #uuid "99d98916-c136-5f7e-898c-502b466106fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "edfe2065-c6c9-560e-b435-e28eacd51b92" :question/math-academy-id "q-18451" :question/problem "We wish to construct a cylindrical can with a base and a lid. Our cylinder must have an exact volume of $686\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{3}.$ What is the smallest surface we can get?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/ce/cec96bdd2335ff5635d11c4ea6016257b35bd5450778a70ecc1f5e0156ccfe9e.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi rh+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $686\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n686\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{686}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(r) & =2\\pi r(\\frac{686}{{r}^{2}})+2\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,372\\pi }{r}+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $S,$ so we need to differentiate $S$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{1\\,372\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1\\,372\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 4\\pi r & =\\frac{1\\,372\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{3} & =1\\,372\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =343 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{343} \\\\  & =7\\,\\text{mm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<7$ then ${S}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>7$ then ${S}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=7$ is indeed a minimum of $S(r).$\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the smallest surface area has radius $r=7\\,\\text{mm}.$\n\nFinally, to find the smallest surface area, we substitute $r=7\\,\\text{mm}$ into the equation for the surface area $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(7) & =\\frac{1\\,372\\pi }{7}+2\\pi (7)^{2} \\\\  & =294\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the smallest possible surface area of the cylinder is $S=294\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510476 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9596c44c-aa01-5bd0-a490-4163ead1bd61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9596c44c-aa01-5bd0-a490-4163ead1bd61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510003} :answer-field/id #uuid "926323cc-4935-53fe-b3b6-46d63fc874f8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f9befb55-60b4-591d-bab5-6cd215964711" :question/math-academy-id "q-315673" :question/problem "A manufacturer wishes to build a cylindrical container with a base but no lid. The container must have an exact volume of $9\\pi \\,{ft}^{3}.$\nTo make the container, the manufacturer uses two materials:\n\n- a plastic sheet that costs $$4.50$ per ${ft}^{2}$ for the lateral surface,\n\n- a plastic sheet that costs $$1.50$ per ${ft}^{2}$ for the base.\n\nWhat is the radius $r$ of the container that gives the smallest manufacturing cost? Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/63/6393c2cbff5d727841ca78ac067ff7067114d6db8754454a30584f38e4a4ea87.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the manufacturing cost. We can express the manufacturing cost $C$ in terms of $r$ and $h$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi rh\\cdot 4.50+\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot 1.50 \\\\  & =9\\pi rh+1.5\\pi {r}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $9\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n9\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{9}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(r) & =9\\pi r(\\frac{9}{{r}^{2}})+1.5\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{81\\pi }{r}+1.5\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $C,$ so we need to differentiate $C$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{81\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+3\\pi r.\n$$\n\n \n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{81\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+3\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 3\\pi r & =\\frac{81\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 3\\pi {r}^{3} & =81\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =27 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<3$ then ${C}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>3$ then ${C}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=3$ is indeed a minimum of $C(r).$\n\nTherefore, the container with the smallest manufacturing cost has radius $r=3\\,\\text{ft}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510569 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a997286f-4db9-53ee-8849-22966e083d6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{113}{280}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a997286f-4db9-53ee-8849-22966e083d6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{113}{280}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510578} :answer-field/id #uuid "aac06ef3-2984-5073-a683-edc5d64f81a8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510579}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "107b0d08-c0c2-5e3b-a247-c4238dbd6d8f" :question/problem "Calculate $det(A)$ given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{8} & \\frac{9}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{9} & -\\frac{9}{7}\\end{aligned}].$\n\n$det(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}|=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{8} & \\frac{9}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{9} & -\\frac{9}{7}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}\\cdot (-\\frac{9}{7})-\\frac{9}{5}\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{9}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{45}{56}-(-\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{113}{280}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510573 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32e54747-95d9-5771-963b-49dcf4295948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{55}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32e54747-95d9-5771-963b-49dcf4295948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{55}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510574} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b44817c-904a-519b-9cfd-76497778888f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "295bc951-4997-5df9-ab67-20bc32e8016a" :question/problem "Calculate $det(A)$ given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{7} & -\\frac{9}{5} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}].$\n\n$det(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}|=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{7} & -\\frac{9}{5} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{7}\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{5})-(-\\frac{9}{5})\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7}-(-\\frac{9}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{55}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510575 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "782fb4f6-27a0-5506-9beb-555ca18e4273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1784}{1323}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "782fb4f6-27a0-5506-9beb-555ca18e4273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1784}{1323}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510577} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2dbf444-3856-5b85-8fac-b9843fa4dd21" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4f9be678-4285-5f11-ba52-fd42fc00b682" :question/problem "Calculate $det(A)$ given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{9} & -\\frac{4}{7} \\\\ \\frac{2}{7} & -\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}].$\n\n$det(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}|=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{9} & -\\frac{4}{7} \\\\ \\frac{2}{7} & -\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{9}\\cdot (-\\frac{8}{3})-(-\\frac{4}{7})\\cdot \\frac{2}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{27}-(-\\frac{8}{49}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1784}{1323}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0e44b6c-314a-5501-9b95-06b4d75b5730" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{513}{140}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0e44b6c-314a-5501-9b95-06b4d75b5730" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{513}{140}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510581} :answer-field/id #uuid "23459979-cbd9-5238-a961-af2acea3f96e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "554f7f1f-b6ff-5426-9de0-5f0d3de16c53" :question/problem "Calculate $det(A)$ given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7} & \\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{9}{5} & -\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}].$\n\n$det(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}|=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7} & \\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{9}{5} & -\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\cdot (-\\frac{5}{4})-\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{9}{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{28}-(-\\frac{21}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{513}{140}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510727 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "249f6d39-c352-5b6e-b0a3-aad8dbe4af0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510728} {:answer/id #uuid "40e4413d-62c5-5cf3-8a6d-bc0951e1d6d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{83}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510731} {:answer/id #uuid "9ae139e3-a55e-5972-9da7-3d735e750073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510738} {:answer/id #uuid "cf16047a-f770-52e9-8ba0-da97bd2d6088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{258}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510745} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa164a1-1ef9-5a0d-9231-f146dcc770a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{258}}{43}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510747}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf16047a-f770-52e9-8ba0-da97bd2d6088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{258}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510745} :answer-field/id #uuid "af481ffe-765a-562a-87bb-3829101afbf0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2014768a-a233-5eee-b9c5-afc2675420b0" :question/problem "![](images/cc/ccdb5bb029197875aaeebfc899dff4d25597b657e64ea95d1b34d09df0a37789.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $tan⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/af/af413350ef3c9edde0eebf49cb8f37c8b20791f4bb01e04adaacaa0c378d28a1.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{43}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{6}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7}.$\n\nKnowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{\\sqrt{6}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{43\\cdot 6}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{258}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510730 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1909811c-da8d-539b-aa89-cdc5b420a83f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510725} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc3517e-1300-5538-b247-d948b579f7f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510729} {:answer/id #uuid "ec9189a3-d0b3-5483-9c84-198750079c85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{58}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510748} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b2d445-8bcf-5fd5-a74d-dc7c7c42cc23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510750} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e48c42-ff53-5bcc-b008-e6ccbde37ace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3b2d445-8bcf-5fd5-a74d-dc7c7c42cc23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510750} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5c54992-c621-521f-8066-259a4e4e6c43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c08d8db-de56-5ccc-8319-0573cd7cc4f0" :question/problem "![](images/d0/d04fe72eb2ad53b69837aaf402addd75d9b087225d70e876233e23092fc85764.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $tan⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/51/51778a4029b91dbbd51014a8f4a3e7e1fc772efe96058f41b21bbf408f113cce.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{18}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{5})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{\\sqrt{7}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{18}}{\\sqrt{7}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{18\\cdot 7}}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{126}}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510733 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19cd8750-e5e7-52ce-b115-cbd111d3d902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{390}}{39}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510726} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba5953e-1bc3-52b3-949b-b1a3372ec514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{390}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510734} {:answer/id #uuid "dca87888-b247-5218-bd97-b52279791479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510746} {:answer/id #uuid "ed349bbe-0002-555d-b774-e4baa909f28c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{79}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510749} {:answer/id #uuid "f51c1080-9d67-5d5c-9ad2-f5bee4c4e810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ba5953e-1bc3-52b3-949b-b1a3372ec514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\fr