\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{\\sqrt{10}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{39\\cdot 10}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{390}}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510744 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bc4984a-76a7-5e40-bec6-4baeb697eb93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510732} {:answer/id #uuid "93b2d161-28c4-50ff-962d-90ee2fbdac17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510737} {:answer/id #uuid "a332604f-d236-5a81-8925-b96c1993b9c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510739} {:answer/id #uuid "b382a9d0-fd0f-530d-8c12-c535c8651899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510741} {:answer/id #uuid "c8cc011f-2ad6-5dc2-af67-11ebde52edbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93b2d161-28c4-50ff-962d-90ee2fbdac17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510737} :answer-field/id #uuid "68af0948-d377-54ba-bc95-3bf16b5f82fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cab2c352-408a-5716-9c82-d09e82069fc3" :question/problem "![](images/dd/dd9061a8ef1b2f0d8fe9d730f18c5e10bef42a9acd33f23d36efb3728ce0614b.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{15}{17},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $tan⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/ba/bad705982f073623644ed540e26cfad849b2dd078c5c64772a0be3937d618a27.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{15}{17}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{15}{17}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{15}{17}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{15}{17})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{225}{289}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{64}{289}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{8}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{8}{17}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{15}{17})}{(\\frac{8}{17})}=\\frac{15}{8}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511461 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13a86203-0bc5-55e2-986d-c1c309e6ee38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510822} {:answer/id #uuid "17d0dd42-5a1b-52aa-becb-f58c46712b06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510835} {:answer/id #uuid "560d4297-737e-51ee-b485-c82f9de8306b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510965} {:answer/id #uuid "84972923-41cf-5a0d-9170-7092398d68bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511055} {:answer/id #uuid "93fd1a29-955a-551f-9dee-8f81ad48e8f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No such $k$ exists" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13a86203-0bc5-55e2-986d-c1c309e6ee38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510822} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee0499b8-2a39-5262-89e9-bfc3b9bddd29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0a4ad86f-33ac-54b8-ac3c-2f4008b70bee" :question/math-academy-id "q-48751" :question/problem "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what values of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=-3?$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+14x+33}{2kx+6k}, & x<-3 \\\\ -\\frac{kx}{3}, & x\\ge -3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $g(x)$, the left-sided limit of $g(x)$ at $x=-3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}\\frac{{x}^{2}+14x+33}{2kx+6k} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}\\frac{(x+11)(x+3)}{2k(x+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}\\frac{(x+11)\\cancel{(x+3)}}{2k\\cancel{(x+3)}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}\\frac{x+11}{2k} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}(-\\frac{kx}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{k(-3)}{3} \\\\  & =k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $g(-3)=k.$ \n\nSo, for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-3,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}g(x) \\\\ k & =\\frac{4}{k} \\\\ {k}^{2} & =4 \\\\ k & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=-2$ or $k=2$ remove the discontinuity at $x=-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511492 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d3de7cb-dc94-55e8-a78d-a27f24ecf883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{4}+1}}{{\\theta }^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510875} {:answer/id #uuid "5df30dae-9f1b-5169-85f4-352ef7b35830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+1}}{2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510978} {:answer/id #uuid "8b872d30-3b7e-5aae-91f8-0a201257e3ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+1}}{{\\theta }^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511071} {:answer/id #uuid "c81c2c1a-18d2-5fae-8d05-046e1ef9709a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{\\theta }}{\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511197} {:answer/id #uuid "f3baf0b1-8376-594a-b0c2-adace7164539" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{2\\sqrt{\\theta }}{{\\theta }^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b872d30-3b7e-5aae-91f8-0a201257e3ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+1}}{{\\theta }^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511071} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ddef5b4-7fd6-526e-90d2-54235e050d54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4cb23656-32ad-5111-abf0-812956e034d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-149800" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\frac{1}{\\theta }$ for $1\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\,?$" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\frac{1}{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{\\theta })}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{2}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}+1}{{\\theta }^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+1}}{{\\theta }^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511494 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a0018a6-7dae-54cf-88b4-02338b8fff5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510914} {:answer/id #uuid "a218fed0-75a7-50d9-8f60-c86aeaebfd23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511121} {:answer/id #uuid "a24957bf-c3bd-5b14-b0b3-55b5967d855a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511123} {:answer/id #uuid "bd5b03f7-2a8e-557c-abde-00dafe7a50cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511173} {:answer/id #uuid "e533b56d-0765-5385-b5e3-a0e0dfcc5a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a0018a6-7dae-54cf-88b4-02338b8fff5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510914} :answer-field/id #uuid "29cd7cba-3362-549f-8e3e-97da0f1c1325" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d6df4b5-e301-58fd-845a-847c7a249d39" :question/math-academy-id "q-140849" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{13}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511532 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "864ead19-4057-5f6a-8c06-48fb2c7562de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511059} {:answer/id #uuid "9dd05ee6-ac91-5d21-8ecf-c0b6a272af81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "discontinuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "864ead19-4057-5f6a-8c06-48fb2c7562de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511059} :answer-field/id #uuid "0de15137-bf56-596a-a38a-ac01796200ee" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511317} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e66d128-cec6-555a-8143-73ec6e9676c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510878} {:answer/id #uuid "8e23dd8e-fbc6-5790-9b1d-fe0b23c91599" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511075} {:answer/id #uuid "df2fadf9-ffc9-50bc-80ad-3b43d5a65713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511244} {:answer/id #uuid "e374467d-7122-514c-8ff6-30b09a2ff344" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df2fadf9-ffc9-50bc-80ad-3b43d5a65713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math}